**Describe the bug**
When a context manager's `__exit__()` function is annotated (or inferred) as returning `None` or `Literal[False]`, pyright (correctly) infers that the entire body of the `with` block must have run for any code after the block, since the context manager (if the typing is correct) won't suppress any exceptions. If the `__exit__()` function is annotated (or inferred) as returning `bool` or `Literal[True]`, then it (correctly) infers that some or none of the body of the `with` block may have run if there was an exception that was suppressed.

